
########## density.py ##########

1. The two densities in one sentence

  [P] plough depth                                 0.30 m
      soil volume under 1 m2                    300,000 cm3

  [P] "1 pellet per 5 cm3" implies               60,000 pellets/m2
  [P] "approximately 2,500 pellets per 1 m2"       2,500 pellets/m2
      ratio                                        24.0 x

      2,500 pellets/m2 corresponds to 1 pellet per 120 cm3, not 5.

2. Which of the two the rest of the paper uses

  [P] 25 t/ha                                      2.50 kg/m2
      mass per pellet at 2,500/m2                1.0000 g
      mass per pellet at 60,000/m2               0.0417 g

      So the mass budget is self-consistent with 2,500 pellets of 1 g each,
      which is a sensible pellet.  The 5 cm3 figure is the one that does not
      fit, and the paper's economics are built on the 2,500.

3. What honouring the 5 cm3 figure would cost

      at the paper's own 1 g per pellet:
        matrix                    600 t/ha      paper:           25 t/ha
        for 30 ha              18,000 t         paper:          750 t
        at the same $/t    $24,000,000           paper:  $1,000,000

      i.e. the sentence's own premise, taken at face value, multiplies the
      case study by 24 and the budget with it.  This is a single arithmetic
      slip in one sentence, not a defect in the concept; what it costs the
      paper is that the physical-contact requirement and the costed design
      are not the same design.

4. What "constant physical contact" comes to at each density

    pellets/m2       per m3   mean spacing  max distance to nearest
         2,500        8,333        4.93 cm                  2.47 cm
        60,000      200,000        1.71 cm                  0.85 cm

      On a cubic lattice at the costed density a root is never more than
      2.5 cm from a pellet, and at the stated density never more than 0.9 cm.
      Neither is contact; both are a distance a root has to grow across, and
      the paper gives no root-length density to compare them against.

5. The one figure in this section that checks out

  [P] Sec. 1: "A plant is capable of assimilating only 30% to 50% of the
      supplied nutrients in real-time"
  [V] "Fertilizer N-recovery efficiency by the first crop is 30 to 50%"
      -- IFA, Nutrient/fertilizer use efficiency, ch. 1 (2014)
      This is correct, and it is the premise the whole paper starts from.

########## isotopes.py ##########

0. Where the constants in this script come from

       12  [P] from the preprint
       20  [V] verified against a named source
        8  [A] assumed range, swept

      Every conclusion drawn from an [A] value below is computed across its
      whole range and stated only where it holds throughout.

1. The spins, which the paper has right

   isotope   spin   natural abundance   where the paper puts it
    La-138      5            0.0888%   synaptic calcium substitute
     Mg-25    5/2            10.000%   chlorophyll, ATP synthase
     Zn-67    5/2             4.100%   zinc fingers
     Ca-43    7/2             0.135%   synaptic calcium

  All four spins are stated correctly in the preprint.  They are non-zero
  because each nuclide has an odd number of either protons or neutrons,
  while the abundant isotopes of the same elements -- Mg-24/26, Zn-64/66/68,
  Ca-40 -- are even-even and therefore have I = 0 exactly.  That contrast is
  what makes the magnetic isotope effect possible, and the paper's reasoning
  up to this point is sound.

2. La-138 is not a stable isotope

  [P] abstract, Sec. 3.3 and the Zenodo keywords all call it stable
  [V] it is a primordial RADIONUCLIDE
        half-life   1.03e+11 years
        decay       beta+ 65.5% to Ba-138,  beta- 34.5% to Ce-138
        stable?     False

  It is one of only a handful of odd-odd nuclides that survive from the
  formation of the Earth, which is exactly why it is rare.  The rarity the
  paper selects for and the instability it overlooks are the same fact.

3. The premise fails for lanthanum before stability even matters

  [P] "the selection of rare, stable isotopes with a non-zero ... nuclear spin"
  [V] La-139, 99.91% of all lanthanum, has spin 7/2

  Non-zero.  So for lanthanum there is nothing to gain by selecting the rare
  isotope on spin grounds: the common one already has one, and a larger one.
  For Mg, Zn and Ca the paper's logic does work, because there the abundant
  isotopes are spinless.  Lanthanum is the element it does not work for, and
  it is the element the section is built around.

4. At natural abundance the matrix adds less than the field already holds

  [P] 1 t La2O3 over 30 ha
      mass fraction La in La2O3                0.8527
      lanthanum added                           2.842 g/m2

  [V] soil La at 31 ppm (Rudnick & Gao), swept over bulk density:
          bulk density    soil mass    La present  increase   new ppm
             1.1 g/cm3       330 kg    10.23 g/m2     27.8%      39.6
             1.4 g/cm3       420 kg    13.02 g/m2     21.8%      37.8
             1.6 g/cm3       480 kg    14.88 g/m2     19.1%      36.9

      So the 'most radical innovation' raises the field's lanthanum by about
      a fifth, and -- since the paper never says the lanthanum is
      isotopically enriched -- leaves the La-138 fraction exactly where it
      was, at 0.0888 %.  A natural isotope ratio is not something a soil can
      be deficient in.

5. The same test for the other two isotopes named

      [A] soil contents are swept, not asserted; see constants.py
       isotope      element in soil, g/m2      isotope present, g/m2
         Mg-25       840.0 -     12600.0         84.00 -     1260.00
         Zn-67         8.4 -        50.4          0.34 -        2.07

      Chlorophyll already contains Mg-25 at 10.00 % of its magnesium and
      zinc-finger proteins already contain Zn-67 at 4.10 % of their zinc.
      Across the whole swept range the quantity already in the ploughed
      layer exceeds anything the matrix adds by orders of magnitude.  The
      magnetic isotope effect, whatever its size, is already running.

6. What enrichment would have cost instead

      to obtain 1 t of La-138 at 0.0888% natural abundance
      requires      1,126 t of natural lanthanum as feed

      The paper's budget has 1 t of lanthanum oxide at a price consistent
      with ordinary commercial material, and never uses the word enriched.
      Both readings are therefore unavailable to it at once: unenriched, the
      Quantum Circuit adds nothing; enriched, the budget is wrong by orders
      of magnitude.

7. Radiologically the added La-138 is negligible, and that is in the
   paper's favour

      La-138 in 1 t of natural La2O3               757.3 g
      decay constant                          2.132e-19 /s
      specific activity of pure La-138            931.2 Bq/g
      total activity added over 30 ha               705.2 kBq
      areal activity                               2.35 Bq/m2

      [A] against the potassium-40 already in the soil, swept:
        K-40 at   100 Bq/kg ->      42,000 Bq/m2    added / present = 5.6e-05
        K-40 at   700 Bq/kg ->     294,000 Bq/m2    added / present = 8.0e-06

      Between 4 and 5 orders of magnitude below the natural potassium
      background, across the whole range.  Calling La-138 a stable isotope
      is a factual error about the nuclide, not a safety problem with the
      proposal, and the distinction is worth keeping.

8. Gold loading, for scale

  [P] 5 kg over 30 ha                    16.667 mg/m2
      at bulk density 1.1 g/cm3                  50.5 ppb
      at bulk density 1.6 g/cm3                  34.7 ppb
      [A] crustal gold is quoted as 1-4 ppb,
          unverified here, so this is scale only and carries no conclusion.

########## economics.py ##########

1. The gold line against the spot price on the date of deposit

  [V] spot, 6 October 2026           $  4,168.80/troy oz  = $134.03/g
  [P] $417,000 for 5 kg implies      $  2,594.03/troy oz  = $ 83.40/g

      5 kg at spot                     $     670,150
      shortfall in that one line       $     253,150   (1.61 x)
      total capex restated             $   1,253,150

      The implied price is roughly what gold cost two years before the
      preprint was written, so this reads as a stale figure rather than an
      error of method.  It moves the headline total by a quarter.

2. The return, on the paper's own numbers

  [P] capital expenditure              $   1,000,000
  [P] annual saving                    $      25,000
      simple payback, undiscounted                40 years
      capex per hectare                $      33,333 /ha

      break-even discount rate in perpetuity   2.50% per year

      [A]   rate     PV of the saving              NPV
           0.0%            unbounded        unbounded
           1.0% $          2,500,000 $      1,500,000
           2.0% $          1,250,000 $        250,000
           2.5% $          1,000,000 $              0
           3.0% $            833,333 $       -166,667
           5.0% $            500,000 $       -500,000
           7.0% $            357,143 $       -642,857

      The investment is positive only at a real discount rate at or below
      2.5 % held in perpetuity.  The paper calls it 'permanent capital',
      which is the right frame for the claim -- but the claim then needs the
      inventory to actually be permanent, which is the next section.

3. How much leakage the closed loop can take

      The loop is only closed if the metals return.  Phytomining works by
      concentrating metal into harvested biomass, and biomass leaves the
      field, so the return fraction is the whole question.  The paper assumes
      it is exactly 1.

      [A]  annual loss   half the inventory gone in
                 0.1%                    692.8 years
                 0.5%                    138.3 years
                 1.0%                     69.0 years
                 2.0%                     34.3 years
                 5.0%                     13.5 years
                10.0%                      6.6 years

      For more than half the inventory to survive to the 40-year
      payback, the annual loss has to stay below 1.72%.

      That is a demanding number for an open field: it has to cover harvest
      removal net of returned manure, leaching, erosion and runoff, all at
      once.  The paper gives no figure for any of them, so this is a bound
      on what the claim requires rather than a measurement of what happens.

4. What the comparison omits

  [P] the saving is 'synthetic fertilizers, pesticides, and associated
      diesel fuel' at $25,000/yr for 30 ha

      Not costed anywhere in the paper, and each would move the result:
        - replacement of the pellet matrix as biochar mineralises
        - the yield difference, positive or negative, which is never measured
        - the cost of monitoring metal levels in food crops
        - any regulatory cost of putting 5 kg of gold and 1 t of lanthanum oxide into farmland

      The first matters most to the 'permanent capital' claim: biochar is
      the carrier for everything else, and the cited Lehmann review treats
      its persistence in soil as an open question rather than a given.
